ll the  difference between molecules that are in  your own cells and these molecules that  are in bacteria and viruses that are  foreign  and so what those people then make and  it's a rare disorder is antibodies  against their own cellular components  which are called Auto antibodies and  that's why these are known as autoimmune  diseases and what happens if you have  Auto antibodies let's think about it if  they're antibodies against your own  cellular components then let's say you  go out in the sunshine and you get a  sunburn that kills your cells or not a  lot but some of your skin cells and they  die and they dump their contents into  your blood so if you have Auto  antibodies then you get these  interactions between your antibodies  your Auto antibodies and your own  cellular components to form what are  called immune complexes and these things  build up in big networks sort of like we  saw with the DNA in your blood stream  and the reason that the disease is  called lupus is people get a red rash  and that's because a lot of these  complexes have deposited in the little  capillaries that are under the skin okay  the complex is also depositing the hair  follicles so people's hair falls out  they deposit in the joint so people  can't move very well they deposit  actually in all the internal organs  these are systemic diseases and very  very difficult to treat and a lot of  people end up succumbing to kidney  disease because the immune complexes  deposit preferentially in the kidneys  and those are actually antibodies  against DNA so so that's the next  question is what is it that in these  diseases people make auto antibodies  against and it turns out for reasons we  don't understand that there's some of  the most abundant and highly conserved  things inside cells okay how many of you  kids have heard DNA makes RNA and RNA  makes protein very large fraction of  who's here right okay so here's a  picture of DNA this is a strand of DNA  they're polymerase is moving along  making RNA so strands of RNA and those  are getting coated with ribosomes which  are the protein making machinery inside  the cells so here you're seeing DNA  makes RNA makes protein and in fact some  patients with lupus make antibodies  against ribosomes protein synthesis  factories many people with lupus make  antibodies against their own DNA and  that's what deposits and the kidneys and  causes the real problems but many also  make antibodies against snips no slurps  aren't in this picture because this is a  picture of what goes on in bacteria and  bacteria don't have snips but our cells  have streams so what are snips why do we  have them and this has to do with the  sense and the nonsense in our genes so  let me move on to how did how did I get  into this whole area  here we're looking at DNA right and  we're looking at the amount of DNA and  you can tell how much there is by you  know what a big pile of strands there  are there and after this very time when  everybody thought you had to be very  simple some molecular biologists started  thinking well we'd better start looking  at higher cells and most people said oh  they'll be just the same but some people  said maybe they'll be different and we  now know they're very different in terms  of our DNA so what this slide shows is  here's a virus and it has about 200  genes and that's the amount of DNA  here's a bacterium it has about 2,000  genes so does that look like about 10  times as much DNA yep this is a  mammalian cell a huge amount of DNA and  people used to think we had about a  hundred thousand genes do any of the  kids know how many genes we think we  have now but billions No  anybody know anybody else know we have  about probably about 27,000 genes anyhow  this amount of DNA was way more than  what we would need for twenty thousand  seven thousand genes about a thousand  times more and this was very puzzling to  people was also true that mammalian  cells made lots and lots of RNA copying  t