ee what's called a spliceosome  and here the whole nonsense bit the  intron is looped out and it's about to  get cut and rejoined and made shorter  and you've gotten rid of the nonsense  okay so that's what's going on in your  cells all the time has to happen  virtually any time a gene is turned on  it makes RNA but that RNA has got  introns in it and it's got to get  spliced by this process so let's think  about what that means okay so what it  means is that our cells have this extra  step in making proteins they make the  RNA but it's got nonsense in it and the  nonsense has to come out by the SERPs  finding the junctions and making a  spliceosome and then making a messenger  RNA so let me show you I think okay so  thinking about this and now moving  forward this was a number of years ago  what does this mean for our  understanding and what does it mean for  disease virtually all of our genes have  this exon intron exon structure sense  nonsense sense nonsense etc and what  that means is that they're different  ways of splicing the RNA coming off a  gene so that you can either make a  protein that has the red bit or a  protein that doesn't have the red bit  they're different proteins and turns out  that Drosophila has about the same  number of genes the little fruit fly as  we do but we do more of this stuff this  alternative splicing so we can get more  out of that same number of genes and  make more proteins and be more  complicated so that's important in terms  of understanding evolution the other  in terms of understanding disease there  are a lot of diseases where the splicing  process goes awry in a particular gene  and their diseases like cystic fibrosis  and spinal muscular atrophy muscular  dystrophy where people are now trying to  figure out how to correct the splicing  process in those genes and some progress  is being made because what you need to  do is get out of it something that can  actually a messenger RNA that can make  protein and I'm just comi